.DS_Store
xcuserdata/
*.xcodeproj
/.build
/DerivedData
.swiftpm/

# Raw device-test artifacts (drive logs, sqlite dumps, personal photos) stay local
Docs/tests/

# **Real trip dumps never enter the repo** (privacy principle, CLAUDE.md §0).
# `Tools/exif-to-fixture.sh` writes here, and the render harnesses prefer it over
# the committed synthetic fixture of the same name. A whole-directory rule on
# purpose: a filename pattern is one `mytrip.json` away from being defeated, and
# what leaks is a person's movements, not a build artifact.
Tests/Fixtures/trips/local/

# Secrets and local overrides, anywhere in the tree. Broadened 2026-08-16: the
# rule was `Deploy/.env` only, so a `.env` at the repo root was tracked-able —
# and a routing provider's API key is the next thing that will live in one.
# `*.example` templates stay committed; they are what the rule documents.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Build-time secrets. The routing API key reaches the app through an xcconfig
# that is generated locally from ~/.kamome/routing.env — it must never be
# committed, and Secrets.xcconfig.example is the template that documents it.
Config/Secrets.xcconfig
!Config/Secrets.xcconfig.example

# Wrangler's local secret file for `npx wrangler dev`. Same class as
# Secrets.xcconfig, and added 2026-08-28 alongside the Worker README's debug
# runbook, which now names it: `.dev.vars` holds a plaintext GEOAPIFY_API_KEY,
# Wrangler does not ignore it for you, and `RoutingKeyTests` guards the config
# path rather than the filesystem — so nothing else here would have caught it.
.dev.vars
**/.dev.vars
!**/.dev.vars.example

# Built routing + tile artifacts are gigabytes and live outside the repo
# ($KAMOME_DATA_DIR, default ~/kamome-osrm) — this only catches strays.
*.osm.pbf
*.pmtiles
!Tests/Fixtures/tiles/*.pmtiles
